Ported from UniXNU patches/mesa/turnip-darwin-kmd.patch (BSD-2-Clause-Patent),
commits c7353ea "red-black tree GPU address allocator and a size-class BO
cache", 83cce1d "reap every completed BO, not only the oldest", caffd5d
"release cached BOs on OS memory pressure" and b624df5 "free cached BOs not
reused within a second": the file as of b624df5, less the parts listed below.
The pinned UniXNU revision fb6aad6 predates them. Applied after
mesa-fuxi-cached-coherent.patch.

turnip/darwin: keep freed BOs whole in a size-class cache, give out GPU
addresses from two red-black trees, and reap every completed BO.

A BO whose fence completed keeps its kernel handle, GPU mapping and CPU lease
in a cache keyed by size class (4 KiB steps up to 16 KiB, then quarters of a
power of two), GPU permissions and, on Fuxi, the cached-coherent attribute.
The next allocation of that class takes it (a cached BO may be up to a quarter
larger than the request) without BoAlloc, BoMap (the SMMU update, about 1 ms on
UniXNU), BoFree or the unmap. Replayable BOs and host imports never enter it; device-internal BOs are
zeroed on reuse. Entries not reused within a second go back to the kernel, and
everything goes back on a libdispatch memory-pressure warning and when BoAlloc
or a GPU range fails (that allocation is then tried once more). The retire ring
and the cache grow instead of stopping at 256 entries, and a BO behind a fence
still in flight no longer holds back completed ones queued after it.
TU_DARWIN_BO_CACHE=0 turns the cache off. Every 4096 allocations stderr gets
'NativeAdreno BO cache: N allocations, N reused (P%), ...'.

Not taken: b252d39 and the copy-back hunk of 83cce1d (they need
NativeAdrenoParamsOwnedCoherent, which our ABI lacks; mesa-fuxi-cached-coherent.patch
is our equivalent), bdcf746's pool-pressure poll (NativeAdrenoParamsPoolPressure,
removed again by caffd5d), 2c40ae9 (skipping CPU waits relies on UniXNU's ring
prologue), and caffd5d's unbounded idle and ring-space waits: once our 100 ms
watchdog poisons the GPU, WaitFence answers kIOReturnTimeout at once and for
good (fuxi_gpu_abi_poll -3/-4), so those waits keep their 5 s bound and 64
submit attempts and still end in device loss.

